<H1 class=3DTitle><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>8<BR><A=20
name=3DPFGRF008>Using the Rule-Based =
Optimizer</A></FONT></H1><!--/TOC=3DTitle--><A=20
name=3D28908></A>
<P class=3DBP>This chapter discusses Oracle's rule-based optimizer =
(RBO). In=20
general, always use the cost-based approach. The rule-based approach is=20
available for backward compatibility.</P><A name=3D43761></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D43787></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D43788></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Oracle Corporation strongly advises the use of =
cost-based=20
      optimization. Rule-based optimization will be deprecated in a =
future=20
      release.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D41321></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D41324></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH><STRONG=20
      class=3DBold>See Also</STRONG>:</STRONG></FONT> <A =
name=3D41325></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#721">Chapter&nbsp;1,=20
      "Introduction to the =
Optimizer"</A></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D29099></A>
<P class=3DBP>This chapter contains the following sections:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41298></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38864">Overview=20
  of the Rule-Based Optimizer (RBO)</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41302></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893">Understanding=20
  Access Paths for the RBO</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D42310></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#41432">Transforming=20
  and Optimizing Statements with the RBO</A> </LI></UL><A =
name=3D38864></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"38864"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Overview of=20
the Rule-Based Optimizer (RBO)</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A =
name=3D38865></A>
<P class=3DBP>Although Oracle supports the rule-based optimizer, you =
should design=20
new applications to use the cost-based optimizer (CBO). You should also =
use the=20
CBO for data warehousing applications, because the CBO supports enhanced =

features for DSS. Many new performance features, such as partitioned =
tables,=20
improved star query processing, and materialized views, are only =
available with=20
the CBO.</P><A name=3D38873></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D38868></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D38871></A>
      <P class=3DNB>If you have developed OLTP applications using Oracle =
version=20
      6, and if you have tuned the SQL statements carefully based on the =
rules=20
      of the optimizer, then you might want to continue using the RBO =
when you=20
      upgrade these applications to a new Oracle release. <A =
name=3D38872></A></P>
      <P class=3DNB>If you are using applications provided by =
third-party vendors,=20
      then check with the vendors to determine which type of optimizer =
is best=20
      suited to that application.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D38874></A>
<P class=3DBP>If <CODE>OPTIMIZER_MODE</CODE>=3D<CODE>CHOOSE</CODE>, if =
statistics do=20
not exist, and if you do not add hints to SQL statements, then SQL =
statements=20
use the RBO. You can use the RBO to access both relational data and =
object=20
types. If <CODE>OPTIMIZER_MODE</CODE>=3D<CODE>FIRST_ROWS</CODE>,=20
<CODE>FIRST_ROWS_</CODE><EM class=3DItalic>n</EM>, or =
<CODE>ALL_ROWS</CODE> and no=20
statistics exist, then the CBO uses default statistics. Migrate existing =

applications to use the cost-based approach.</P><A name=3D38875></A>
<P class=3DBP>You can enable the CBO on a trial basis simply by =
collecting=20
statistics. You can then return to the RBO by deleting the statistics or =
by=20
setting either the value of the <CODE>OPTIMIZER_MODE</CODE> =
initialization=20
parameter or the <CODE>OPTIMIZER_MODE</CODE> clause of the =
<CODE>ALTER</CODE>=20
<CODE>SESSION</CODE> statement to <CODE>RULE</CODE>. You can also use =
this value=20
if you want to collect and examine statistics for data without using the =

cost-based approach.</P><A name=3D38891></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D38880></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH><STRONG=20
      class=3DBold>See Also</STRONG>:</STRONG></FONT> <A =
name=3D38881></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#13547">Chapter&nbsp;3,=20
      "Gathering Optimizer Statistics"</A> for an explanation of how to =
gather=20
      statistics</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A =
name=3D38893></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"38893"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Understanding Access Paths for the =
RBO</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A=20
name=3D38896></A>
<P class=3DBP>Using the RBO, the optimizer chooses an execution plan =
based on the=20
access paths available and the ranks of these access paths. Oracle's =
ranking of=20
the access paths is heuristic. If there is more than one way to execute =
a SQL=20
statement, then the RBO always uses the operation with the lower rank. =
Usually,=20
operations of lower rank execute faster than those associated with =
constructs of=20
higher rank.</P><A name=3D38897></A>
<P class=3DBP>The list shows access paths and their ranking:</P><A =
name=3D38901></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38960">RBO=20
Path 1: Single Row by Rowid</A></P><A name=3D38905></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38979">RBO=20
Path 2: Single Row by Cluster Join</A></P><A name=3D38909></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39019">RBO=20
Path 3: Single Row by Hash Cluster Key with Unique or Primary =
Key</A></P><A=20
name=3D38913></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39041">RBO=20
Path 4: Single Row by Unique or Primary Key</A></P><A name=3D38917></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39063">RBO=20
Path 5: Clustered Join</A></P><A name=3D38921></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39095">RBO=20
Path 6: Hash Cluster Key</A></P><A name=3D38925></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39114">RBO=20
Path 7: Indexed Cluster Key</A></P><A name=3D38929></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39135">RBO=20
Path 8: Composite Index</A></P><A name=3D38933></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39156">RBO=20
Path 9: Single-Column Indexes</A></P><A name=3D38937></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39197">RBO=20
Path 10: Bounded Range Search on Indexed Columns</A></P><A =
name=3D38941></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39232">RBO=20
Path 11: Unbounded Range Search on Indexed Columns</A></P><A =
name=3D38945></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39269">RBO=20
Path 12: Sort Merge Join</A></P><A name=3D38949></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39301">RBO=20
Path 13: MAX or MIN of Indexed Column</A></P><A name=3D38953></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39321">RBO=20
Path 14: ORDER BY on Indexed Column</A></P><A name=3D38957></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#39346">RBO=20
Path 15: Full Table Scan</A></P><A =
name=3D43890></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"43890"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Details of=20
the RBO Access Paths</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D43886></A>
<P class=3DBP>Each of the following sections describes an access path, =
discusses=20
when it is available, and shows the output generated for it by the=20
<CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> statement.</P><A =
name=3D38960></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"38960"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 1:=20
Single Row by Rowid</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D38963></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available only if the statement's=20
<CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause identifies the selected rows by rowid or with =
the=20
<CODE>CURRENT</CODE> <CODE>OF</CODE> <CODE>CURSOR</CODE> embedded SQL =
syntax=20
supported by the Oracle precompilers. To execute the statement, Oracle =
accesses=20
the table by rowid.</P><A name=3D38964></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D38966></A>SELECT =
* FROM emp WHERE ROWID =3D 'AAAA7bAA5AAAA1UAAA';=20
<A name=3D38967></A>
</PRE><A name=3D38969></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D38970></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D38971></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D38972></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D38973></A>    TABLE ACCESS          BY ROWID       EMP=20
</PRE><A name=3D38979></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"38979"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 2:=20
Single Row by Cluster Join</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D38980></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available for statements that join =
tables stored=20
in the same cluster if both of the following conditions are true:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D38981></A>The statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
  clause contains conditions that equate each column of the cluster key =
in one=20
  table with the corresponding column in the other table.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D38982></A>The statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
  clause also contains a condition that guarantees the join returns only =
one=20
  row. Such a condition is likely to be an equality condition on the =
column(s)=20
  of a unique or primary key. </LI></UL><A name=3D38983></A>
<P class=3DBP>These conditions must be combined with <CODE>AND</CODE> =
operators.=20
To execute the statement, Oracle performs a nested loops =
operation.</P><A=20
name=3D38994></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D38986></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D38987></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#77042">"Nested=20
      Loop Outer Joins"</A><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#77042"></A></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D38995></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example, in the following statement, the =
<CODE>emp</CODE> and=20
<CODE>dept</CODE> tables are clustered on the <CODE>deptno</CODE> =
column, and=20
the <CODE>empno</CODE> column is the primary key of the <CODE>emp</CODE> =

table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D38997></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D38998></A>  FROM emp, dept=20
<A name=3D38999></A>  WHERE emp.deptno =3D dept.deptno=20
<A name=3D39000></A>    AND emp.empno =3D 7900;=20
<A name=3D39001></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39003></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39004></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39005></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39006></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39007></A>    NESTED LOOPS=20
<A name=3D39008></A>       TABLE ACCESS       BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39009></A>       INDEX              UNIQUE SCAN    PK_EMP   =20
<A name=3D39010></A>       TABLE ACCESS       CLUSTER        DEPT=20
<A name=3D39011></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39012></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>pk_emp</CODE> is the name of an index that enforces =
the=20
primary key.</P><A name=3D39019></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39019"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 3:=20
Single Row by Hash Cluster Key with Unique or Primary =
Key</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39020></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if both of the following =
conditions=20
are true:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39021></A>The statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
  clause uses all columns of a hash cluster key in equality conditions. =
For=20
  composite cluster keys, the equality conditions must be combined with=20
  <CODE>AND</CODE> operators.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39022></A>The statement is =
guaranteed to=20
  return only one row, because the columns that make up the hash cluster =
key=20
  also make up a unique or primary key. </LI></UL><A name=3D39023></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute the statement, Oracle applies the cluster's =
hash function=20
to the hash cluster key value specified in the statement to obtain a =
hash value.=20
Oracle then uses the hash value to perform a hash scan on the =
table.</P><A=20
name=3D39024></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39025></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, the <CODE>orders</CODE> and=20
<CODE>line_items</CODE> tables are stored in a hash cluster, and the=20
<CODE>orderno</CODE> column is both the cluster key and the primary key =
of the=20
<CODE>orders</CODE> table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39026></A>SELECT =
*=20
<A name=3D39027></A>    FROM orders=20
<A name=3D39028></A>    WHERE orderno =3D 65118968;=20
<A name=3D39029></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39031></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39032></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39033></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39034></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39035></A>    TABLE ACCESS          HASH           ORDERS=20
</PRE><A name=3D39041></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39041"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 4:=20
Single Row by Unique or Primary Key</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D39042></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses all columns of a unique or primary key in equality =
conditions. For=20
composite keys, the equality conditions must be combined with =
<CODE>AND</CODE>=20
operators. To execute the statement, Oracle performs a unique scan on =
the index=20
on the unique or primary key to retrieve a single rowid, and then =
accesses the=20
table by that rowid.</P><A name=3D39043></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39044></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, the <CODE>empno</CODE> column =
is the=20
primary key of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D39045></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39046></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39047></A>    WHERE empno =3D 7900;=20
<A name=3D39048></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39050></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39051></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39052></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39053></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39054></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39055></A>    INDEX                 UNIQUE SCAN    PK_EMP=20
<A name=3D39056></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39057></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>pk_emp</CODE> is the name of the index that enforces =
the=20
primary key.</P><A name=3D39063></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39063"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 5:=20
Clustered Join</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39064></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available for statements that join =
tables stored=20
in the same cluster if the statement's <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause =
contains=20
conditions that equate each column of the cluster key in one table with =
the=20
corresponding column in the other table. For a composite cluster key, =
the=20
equality conditions must be combined with <CODE>AND</CODE> operators. To =
execute=20
the statement, Oracle performs a nested loops operation.</P><A =
name=3D39075></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D39067></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D39068></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#77042">"Nested=20
      Loop Outer Joins"</A><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#77042"></A></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D39076></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39077></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, the <CODE>emp</CODE> and=20
<CODE>dept</CODE> tables are clustered on the <CODE>deptno</CODE> =
column:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39078></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39079></A>    FROM emp, dept=20
<A name=3D39080></A>    WHERE emp.deptno =3D dept.deptno;=20
<A name=3D39081></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39083></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39084></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39085></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39086></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39087></A>  NESTED LOOPS=20
<A name=3D39088></A>    TABLE ACCESS          FULL           DEPT=20
<A name=3D39089></A>    TABLE ACCESS          CLUSTER        EMP=20
</PRE><A name=3D39095></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39095"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 6:=20
Hash Cluster Key</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39096></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses all the columns of a hash cluster key in equality =
conditions. For a=20
composite cluster key, the equality conditions must be combined with=20
<CODE>AND</CODE> operators. To execute the statement, Oracle applies the =

cluster's hash function to the hash cluster key value specified in the =
statement=20
to obtain a hash value. Oracle then uses this hash value to perform a =
hash scan=20
on the table.</P><A name=3D39097></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example: In the following statement, the =
<CODE>orders</CODE> and=20
<CODE>line_items</CODE> tables are stored in a hash cluster, and the=20
<CODE>orderno</CODE> column is the cluster key:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D39099></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39100></A>    FROM line_items=20
<A name=3D39101></A>    WHERE orderno =3D 65118968;=20
<A name=3D39102></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39104></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39105></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39106></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39107></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39108></A>    TABLE ACCESS          HASH           LINE_ITEMS=20
</PRE><A name=3D39114></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39114"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 7:=20
Indexed Cluster Key</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39115></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses all the columns of an indexed cluster key in equality =
conditions.=20
For a composite cluster key, the equality conditions must be combined =
with=20
<CODE>AND</CODE> operators.</P><A name=3D39116></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute the statement, Oracle performs a unique scan on =
the=20
cluster index to retrieve the rowid of one row with the specified =
cluster key=20
value. Oracle then uses that rowid to access the table with a cluster =
scan.=20
Because all rows with the same cluster key value are stored together, =
the=20
cluster scan requires only a single rowid to find them all.</P><A=20
name=3D39117></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39118></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, the <CODE>emp</CODE> table is =
stored in=20
an indexed cluster, and the <CODE>deptno</CODE> column is the cluster =
key:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39119></A>SELECT *  FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39120></A>  WHERE deptno =3D 10;=20
<A name=3D39121></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39123></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39124></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39125></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39126></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39127></A>  TABLE ACCESS            CLUSTER        EMP=20
<A name=3D39128></A>    INDEX                 UNIQUE SCAN    PERS_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39129></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39130></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>pers_index</CODE> is the name of the cluster =
index.</P><A=20
name=3D39135></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39135"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 8:=20
Composite Index</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39136></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses all columns of a composite index in equality conditions =
combined=20
with <CODE>AND</CODE> operators. To execute the statement, Oracle =
performs a=20
range scan on the index to retrieve rowids of the selected rows, and =
then=20
accesses the table by those rowids.</P><A name=3D39137></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39138></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is a composite index on =
the=20
<CODE>job</CODE> and <CODE>deptno</CODE> columns:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D39139></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39140></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39141></A>    WHERE job =3D 'CLERK'=20
<A name=3D39142></A>      AND deptno =3D 30;=20
<A name=3D39143></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39145></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39146></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39147></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39148></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39149></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39150></A>    INDEX                 RANGE SCAN     =
JOB_DEPTNO_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39151></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39152></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>job_deptno_index</CODE> is the name of the composite =
index on=20
the <CODE>job</CODE> and <CODE>deptno</CODE> columns.</P><A =
name=3D39156></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39156"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path 9:=20
Single-Column Indexes</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39157></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses the columns of one or more single-column indexes in equality =

conditions. For multiple single-column indexes, the conditions must be =
combined=20
with <CODE>AND</CODE> operators.</P><A name=3D39158></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause uses the column of only =
one index,=20
then Oracle executes the statement by performing a range scan on the =
index to=20
retrieve the rowids of the selected rows, and then accesses the table by =
these=20
rowids.</P><A name=3D39159></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39160></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is an index on the=20
<CODE>job</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39161></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39162></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39163></A>    WHERE job =3D 'ANALYST';=20
<A name=3D39164></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39166></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39167></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39168></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39169></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39170></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39171></A>    INDEX                 RANGE SCAN     JOB_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39172></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39173></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>job_index</CODE> is the index on=20
<CODE>emp</CODE>.<CODE>job</CODE>.</P><A name=3D39174></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clauses uses columns of many =
single-column=20
indexes, then Oracle executes the statement by performing a range scan =
on each=20
index to retrieve the rowids of the rows that satisfy each condition. =
Oracle=20
then merges the sets of rowids to obtain a set of rowids of rows that =
satisfy=20
all conditions. Oracle then accesses the table using these rowids.</P><A =

name=3D39175></A>
<P class=3DBP>Oracle can merge up to five indexes. If the =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses columns of more than five single-column indexes, then Oracle =
merges=20
five of them, accesses the table by rowid, and then tests the resulting =
rows to=20
determine whether they satisfy the remaining conditions before returning =

them.</P><A name=3D39177></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there are indexes on both the=20
<CODE>job</CODE> and <CODE>deptno</CODE> columns of the <CODE>emp</CODE> =

table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39178></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39179></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39180></A>    WHERE job =3D 'ANALYST'=20
<A name=3D39181></A>      AND deptno =3D 20;=20
<A name=3D39182></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39184></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39185></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39186></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39187></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39188></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39189></A>    AND-EQUAL=20
<A name=3D39190></A>      INDEX               RANGE SCAN     JOB_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39191></A>      INDEX               RANGE SCAN     =
DEPTNO_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39192></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39193></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>AND</CODE>-<CODE>EQUAL</CODE> operation merges =
the rowids=20
obtained by the scans of the <CODE>job_index</CODE> and the=20
<CODE>deptno_index</CODE>, resulting in a set of rowids of rows that =
satisfy the=20
query.</P><A name=3D39197></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39197"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path=20
10: Bounded Range Search on Indexed =
Columns</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D39198></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause contains a condition that uses either the column of a =
single-column index=20
or one or more columns that make up a leading portion of a composite =
index:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39199></A>column =3D =
<EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM>=20
<A name=3D39200></A>
<A name=3D39201></A>column &gt;[=3D] <EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM> AND =
column &lt;[=3D] <EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM>=20
<A name=3D39202></A>
<A name=3D39203></A>column BETWEEN <EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM> AND =
<EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM>=20
<A name=3D39204></A>
<A name=3D39205></A>column LIKE 'c%'=20
<A name=3D39206></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39207></A>
<P class=3DBP>Each of these conditions specifies a bounded range of =
indexed values=20
that are accessed by the statement. The range is said to be bounded =
because the=20
conditions specify both its least value and its greatest value. To =
execute such=20
a statement, Oracle performs a range scan on the index, and then =
accesses the=20
table by rowid.</P><A name=3D39208></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is not available if the expression=20
<EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM> references the indexed column.&nbsp;</P><A=20
name=3D39209></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39210></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is an index on the=20
<CODE>sal</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39211></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39212></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39213></A>    WHERE sal BETWEEN 2000 AND 3000;=20
<A name=3D39214></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39216></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39217></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39218></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39219></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39220></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39221></A>    INDEX                 RANGE SCAN     SAL_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39222></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39223></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>sal_index</CODE> is the name of the index on=20
<CODE>emp</CODE>.<CODE>sal</CODE>.</P><A name=3D39225></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is an index on the=20
<CODE>ename</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39226></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39227></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39228></A>    WHERE ename LIKE 'S%';=20
</PRE><A name=3D39232></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39232"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path=20
11: Unbounded Range Search on Indexed =
Columns</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D39233></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause contains one of the following conditions that use either the =
column of a=20
single-column index or one or more columns of a leading portion of a =
composite=20
index:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39234></A>WHERE column &gt;[=3D] =
<EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM>=20
<A name=3D39235></A>
<A name=3D39236></A>WHERE column &lt;[=3D] <EM><CODE>expr</CODE></EM>=20
<A name=3D39237></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39238></A>
<P class=3DBP>Each of these conditions specifies an unbounded range of =
index=20
values accessed by the statement. The range is said to be unbounded, =
because the=20
condition specifies either its least value or its greatest value, but =
not both.=20
To execute such a statement, Oracle performs a range scan on the index, =
and then=20
accesses the table by rowid.</P><A name=3D39239></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39240></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is an index on the=20
<CODE>sal</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39241></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39242></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39243></A>    WHERE sal &gt; 2000;=20
<A name=3D39244></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39246></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39247></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39248></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39249></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39250></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39251></A>    INDEX                 RANGE SCAN     SAL_INDEX=20
<A name=3D39253></A>
</PRE><A name=3D41016></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is a composite index on =
the=20
<CODE>order</CODE> and <CODE>line</CODE> columns of the =
<CODE>line_items</CODE>=20
table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39254></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39255></A>    FROM line_items=20
<A name=3D39256></A>    WHERE order &gt; 65118968;=20
<A name=3D39257></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39258></A>
<P class=3DBP>The access path is available, because the =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause=20
uses the <CODE>order</CODE> column, a leading portion of the =
index.</P><A=20
name=3D39260></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is <EM class=3DItalic>not</EM> available =
in the=20
following statement, in which there is an index on the =
<CODE>order</CODE> and=20
<CODE>line</CODE> columns:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39261></A>SELECT =
*=20
<A name=3D39262></A>    FROM line_items=20
<A name=3D39263></A>    WHERE line &lt; 4;=20
<A name=3D39264></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39265></A>
<P class=3DBP>The access path is not available because the =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause only uses the <CODE>line</CODE> column, which is not a leading =
portion of=20
the index.</P><A name=3D39269></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39269"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path=20
12: Sort Merge Join</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39270></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available for statements that join =
tables that=20
are not stored together in a cluster if the statement's =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause uses columns from each table in equality conditions. To execute =
such a=20
statement, Oracle uses a sort-merge operation. Oracle can also use a =
nested=20
loops operation to execute a join statement.</P><A name=3D39281></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D39273></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D39276></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#39473">"Understanding=20
      Joins"</A><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#39473"></A>=20
      for information on these =
operations</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D39282></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39283></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, the <CODE>emp</CODE> and=20
<CODE>dept</CODE> tables are not stored in the same cluster:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39284></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39285></A>    FROM emp, dept=20
<A name=3D39286></A>    WHERE emp.deptno =3D dept.deptno;=20
<A name=3D39287></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39289></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39290></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39291></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39292></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39293></A>  MERGE JOIN=20
<A name=3D39294></A>    SORT                  JOIN=20
<A name=3D39295></A>      TABLE ACCESS        FULL           EMP=20
<A name=3D39296></A>    SORT                  JOIN=20
<A name=3D39297></A>  TABLE ACCESS            FULL           DEPT=20
</PRE><A name=3D39301></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39301"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path=20
13: MAX or MIN of Indexed Column</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D39302></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available for a <CODE>SELECT</CODE> =
statement,=20
and all of the following conditions are true:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39303></A>The query uses the =
<CODE>MAX</CODE>=20
  or <CODE>MIN</CODE> function to select the maximum or minimum value of =
either=20
  the column of a single-column index or the leading column of a =
composite=20
  index. The index cannot be a cluster index. The argument to the=20
  <CODE>MAX</CODE> or <CODE>MIN</CODE> function can be any expression =
involving=20
  the column, a constant, or the addition operator (+), the =
concatenation=20
  operation (||), or the <CODE>CONCAT</CODE> function.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39304></A>There are no other =
expressions in=20
  the select list.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39305></A>The statement has no=20
  <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause or <CODE>GROUP</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> =
clause.=20
</LI></UL><A name=3D39306></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute the query, Oracle performs a full scan of the =
index to=20
find the maximum or minimum indexed value. Because only this value is =
selected,=20
Oracle need not access the table after scanning the index.</P><A =
name=3D39307></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example, in the following statement, there is an index =
on the=20
<CODE>sal</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39309></A>SELECT MAX(sal) FROM emp;=20
<A name=3D39310></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39312></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D42492></A>  0      =
SELECT STATEMENT Optimizer=3DCHOOSE=20
<A name=3D42493></A>  1    0  SORT (AGGREGATE)=20
<A name=3D42473></A>  2    1    INDEX (FULL SCAN (MIN/MAX)) OF =
'SAL_INDEX' (NON-UNIQUE)
</PRE><A name=3D39321></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39321"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path=20
14: ORDER BY on Indexed Column</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D39322></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available for a <CODE>SELECT</CODE> =
statement,=20
and all of the following conditions are true:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39323></A>The query contains an=20
  <CODE>ORDER</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> clause that uses either the column =
of a=20
  single-column index or a leading portion of a composite index. The =
index=20
  cannot be a cluster index.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39324></A>There is a =
<CODE>PRIMARY</CODE>=20
  <CODE>KEY</CODE> or <CODE>NOT</CODE> <CODE>NULL</CODE> integrity =
constraint=20
  that guarantees that at least one of the indexed columns listed in the =

  <CODE>ORDER</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> clause contains no nulls.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39326></A>The =
<CODE>NLS_SORT</CODE>=20
  initialization parameter is set to <CODE>BINARY</CODE>. </LI></UL><A=20
name=3D39327></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute the query, Oracle performs a range scan of the =
index to=20
retrieve the rowids of the selected rows in sorted order. Oracle then =
accesses=20
the table by these rowids.</P><A name=3D39328></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example:</P><A name=3D39329></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following statement, there is a primary key on the=20
<CODE>empno</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D39330></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39331></A>    FROM emp=20
<A name=3D39332></A>    ORDER BY empno;=20
<A name=3D39333></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39335></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39336></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39337></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39338></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D39339></A>  TABLE ACCESS            BY ROWID       EMP=20
<A name=3D39340></A>    INDEX                 RANGE SCAN     PK_EMP=20
<A name=3D39341></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39342></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>pk_emp</CODE> is the name of the index that enforces =
the=20
primary key. The primary key ensures that the column does not contain=20
nulls.</P><A name=3D39346></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"39346"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>RBO Path=20
15: Full Table Scan</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D39347></A>
<P class=3DBP>This access path is available for any SQL statement, =
regardless of=20
its <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause conditions, except when its =
<CODE>FROM</CODE>=20
clause contains <CODE>SAMPLE</CODE> or <CODE>SAMPLE</CODE>=20
<CODE>BLOCK</CODE>.</P><A name=3D39350></A>
<P class=3DBP>Note that the full table scan is the lowest ranked access =
path on=20
the list. This means that the RBO always chooses an access path that =
uses an=20
index if one is available, even if a full table scan might execute =
faster.</P><A=20
name=3D39353></A>
<P class=3DBP>The following conditions make index access paths =
unavailable:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39354></A>column1 &gt; column2=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39355></A>column1 &lt; column2=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39356></A>column1 &gt;=3D =
column2=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39357></A>column1 &lt;=3D =
column2 </LI></UL><A=20
name=3D39358></A>
<P class=3DBP>where <EM class=3DItalic>column1</EM> and <EM=20
class=3DItalic>column2</EM> are in the same table.</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39359></A>column <CODE>IS</CODE> =

  <CODE>NULL</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39360></A>column <CODE>IS</CODE> =

  <CODE>NOT</CODE> <CODE>NULL</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39361></A>column =
<CODE>NOT</CODE>=20
  <CODE>IN</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39362></A>column !=3D expr=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39363></A>column =
<CODE>LIKE</CODE> '%pattern'=20
  </LI></UL><A name=3D39364></A>
<P class=3DBP>regardless of whether <EM class=3DItalic>column</EM> is =
indexed.</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39365></A>expr =3D expr2 =
</LI></UL><A=20
name=3D39366></A>
<P class=3DBP>where <EM class=3DItalic>expr</EM> is an expression that =
operates on a=20
column with an operator or function, regardless of whether the column is =

indexed.</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39367></A><CODE>NOT</CODE> =
<CODE>EXISTS</CODE>=20
  subquery=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39371></A><CODE>ROWNUM</CODE> =
pseudocolumn in=20
  a view=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D39372></A>Any condition =
involving a column=20
  that is not indexed </LI></UL><A name=3D39373></A>
<P class=3DBP>Any SQL statement that contains only these constructs and =
no others=20
that make index access paths available must use full table scans.</P><A=20
name=3D39374></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example: The following statement uses a full table =
scan to=20
access the <CODE>emp</CODE> table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D39376></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D39377></A>    FROM emp;=20
<A name=3D39378></A>
</PRE><A name=3D39380></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> output for this =
statement=20
might look like this:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D39381></A>OPERATION   =
              OPTIONS        OBJECT_NAME=20
<A =
name=3D39382></A>-----------------------------------------------------=20
<A name=3D39383></A>SELECT STATEMENT=20
<A name=3D38861></A>  TABLE ACCESS            FULL           EMP=20
</PRE><A name=3D41378></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"41378"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Choosing=20
Execution Plans for Joins with the RBO</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A=20
name=3D41410></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D41414></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D41415></A>
      <P class=3DNB>The following considerations apply to both the =
cost-based and=20
      rule-based approaches:</P>
      <UL class=3DNL>
        <LI class=3DNL type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41416></A>The optimizer =
first determines=20
        whether joining two or more of the tables definitely results in =
a row=20
        source containing at most one row. The optimizer recognizes such =

        situations based on <CODE>UNIQUE</CODE> and <CODE>PRIMARY</CODE> =

        <CODE>KEY</CODE> constraints on the tables. If such a situation =
exists,=20
        then the optimizer places these tables first in the join order. =
The=20
        optimizer then optimizes the join of the remaining set of =
tables.=20
        <LI class=3DNL type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41417></A>For join =
statements with outer=20
        join conditions, the table with the outer join operator must =
come after=20
        the other table in the condition in the join order. The =
optimizer does=20
        not consider join orders that violate this rule. </LI></UL>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D41381></A>
<P class=3DBP>With the rule-based approach, the optimizer performs the =
following=20
steps to choose an execution plan for a statement that joins R =
tables:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D41382></A>The optimizer =
generates a set=20
  of R join orders, each with a different table as the first table. The=20
  optimizer generates each potential join order using this algorithm:=20
  <UL class=3DLB2>
    <LI class=3DLB2 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41383></A>To fill each =
position in the join=20
    order, the optimizer chooses the table with the most highly ranked =
available=20
    access path according to the ranks for access paths described in <A=20
    =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893">"Understanding=20
    Access Paths for the RBO"</A><A=20
    =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893"></A>.=20
    The optimizer repeats this step to fill each subsequent position in =
the join=20
    order.=20
    <LI class=3DLB2 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D42520></A>For each table in =
the join order,=20
    the optimizer also chooses the operation with which to join the =
table to the=20
    previous table or row source in the order. The optimizer does this =
by=20
    ranking the sort-merge operation as access path 12 and applying =
these rules:=20

    <UL class=3DLA3>
      <LI class=3DLA3 type=3DSQUARE><A name=3D42521></A>If the access =
path for the=20
      chosen table is ranked 11 or better, then the optimizer chooses a =
nested=20
      loops operation using the previous table or row source in the join =
order=20
      as the outer table.=20
      <LI class=3DLA3 type=3DSQUARE><A name=3D42522></A>If the access =
path for the=20
      table is ranked lower than 12, and if there is an equijoin =
condition=20
      between the chosen table and the previous table or row source in =
join=20
      order, then the optimizer chooses a sort-merge operation.=20
      <LI class=3DLA3 type=3DSQUARE><A name=3D42523></A>If the access =
path for the=20
      chosen table is ranked lower than 12, and if there is not an =
equijoin=20
      condition, then the optimizer chooses a nested loops operation =
with the=20
      previous table or row source in the join order as the outer table. =

    </LI></UL></LI></UL>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D42524></A>The optimizer =
then chooses=20
  among the resulting set of execution plans. The goal of the =
optimizer's choice=20
  is to maximize the number of nested loops join operations in which the =
inner=20
  table is accessed using an index scan. Because a nested loops join =
involves=20
  accessing the inner table many times, an index on the inner table can =
greatly=20
  improve the performance of a nested loops join.=20
  <P><A name=3D41395></A></P>
  <P class=3DBP1>Usually, the optimizer does not consider the order in =
which=20
  tables appear in the <CODE>FROM</CODE> clause when choosing an =
execution plan.=20
  The optimizer makes this choice by applying the following rules=20
  in&nbsp;order:</P>
  <UL class=3DLB2>
    <LI class=3DLB2 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41396></A>The optimizer =
chooses the=20
    execution plan with the fewest nested-loops operations in which the =
inner=20
    table is accessed with a full table scan.=20
    <LI class=3DLB2 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41397></A>If there is a tie, =
then the=20
    optimizer chooses the execution plan with the fewest sort-merge =
operations.=20
    <LI class=3DLB2 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41398></A>If there is still a =
tie, then the=20
    optimizer chooses the execution plan for which the first table in =
the join=20
    order has the most highly ranked access path:=20
    <UL class=3DLA3>
      <LI class=3DLA3 type=3DSQUARE><A name=3D41399></A>If there is a =
tie among=20
      multiple plans whose first tables are accessed by the =
single-column=20
      indexes access path, then the optimizer chooses the plan whose =
first table=20
      is accessed with the most merged indexes.=20
      <LI class=3DLA3 type=3DSQUARE><A name=3D41400></A>If there is a =
tie among=20
      multiple plans whose first tables are accessed by bounded range =
scans,=20
      then the optimizer chooses the plan whose first table is accessed =
with the=20
      greatest number of leading columns of the composite index. =
</LI></UL>
    <LI class=3DLB2 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41401></A>If there is still a =
tie, then the=20
    optimizer chooses the execution plan for which the first table =
appears later=20
    in the query's <CODE>FROM</CODE> clause. </LI></UL></LI></OL><A =
name=3D41432></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"41432"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Transforming and Optimizing Statements with the =
RBO</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A name=3D41434></A>
<P class=3DBP>SQL is a very flexible query language; often, there are =
many=20
statements you could use to achieve the same goal. Sometimes, the =
optimizer=20
transforms one such statement into another that achieves the same goal =
if the=20
second statement can be executed more efficiently.</P><A =
name=3D42321></A>
<P class=3DBP>This section discusses the following topics:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D42322></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#42324">Transforming=20
  ORs into Compound Queries with the RBO</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D42333></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#42251">Using=20
  Alternative SQL Syntax</A> </LI></UL><A =
name=3D42324></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"42324"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Transforming ORs into Compound Queries with the =
RBO</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D41472></A>
<P class=3DBP>If a query contains a <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause with =
multiple=20
conditions combined with <CODE>OR</CODE> operators, then the optimizer=20
transforms it into an equivalent compound query that uses the =
<CODE>UNION</CODE>=20
<CODE>ALL</CODE> set operator if this makes it execute more =
efficiently:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41474></A>If each condition =
individually makes=20
  an index access path available, then the optimizer can make the=20
  transformation. The optimizer chooses an execution plan for the =
resulting=20
  statement that accesses the table multiple times using the different =
indexes,=20
  and then puts the results together.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D41475></A>If any condition =
requires a full=20
  table scan because it does not make an index available, then the =
optimizer=20
  does not transform the statement. The optimizer chooses a full table =
scan to=20
  execute the statement, and Oracle tests each row in the table to =
determine=20
  whether it satisfies any of the conditions.=20
  <P><A name=3D41494></A></P>
  <DIV align=3Dcenter>
  <TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
  cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
  summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
    <TBODY>
    <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
      <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D41480></A><FONT=20
        face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
        Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D44076></A>
        <P class=3DNB><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893">"Understanding=20
        Access Paths for the RBO"</A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893"></A>=20
        and <A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005572">"How=20
        the CBO Transforms ORs into Compound Queries"</A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005572"></A>=20
        for information on access paths and how indexes make them=20
      available</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV></LI></UL><A =
name=3D41514></A>
<P class=3DBP>With the RBO, the optimizer makes this <CODE>UNION</CODE>=20
<CODE>ALL</CODE> transformation, because each component query of the =
resulting=20
compound query can be executed using an index. The RBO assumes that =
executing=20
the compound query using two index scans is faster than executing the =
original=20
query using a full table scan.</P><A =
name=3D42251></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"42251"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Using=20
Alternative SQL Syntax</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D42252></A>
<P class=3DBP>Because SQL is a flexible language, more than one SQL =
statement can=20
meet the needs of an application. Although two SQL statements can =
produce the=20
same result, Oracle might process one faster than the other. You can use =
the=20
results of the <CODE>EXPLAIN</CODE> <CODE>PLAN</CODE> statement to =
compare the=20
execution plans and costs of the two statements and determine which is =
more=20
efficient.</P><A name=3D42255></A>
<P class=3DBP>This example shows the execution plans for two SQL =
statements that=20
perform the same function. Both statements return all the departments in =
the=20
<CODE>dept</CODE> table that have no employees in the <CODE>emp</CODE> =
table.=20
Each statement searches the <CODE>emp</CODE> table with a subquery. =
Assume there=20
is an index, <CODE>deptno_index</CODE>, on the <CODE>deptno</CODE> =
column of the=20
<CODE>emp</CODE> table.</P><A name=3D42258></A>
<P class=3DBP>The first statement and its execution plan:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D42259></A>SELECT dname, deptno
<A name=3D42260></A>   FROM dept
<A name=3D42261></A>   WHERE deptno NOT IN
<A name=3D42262></A>      (SELECT deptno FROM emp);
<A name=3D44245></A>
</PRE><A name=3D44248></A>
<P class=3DBP>The execution plan for the transformed statement might =
look like the=20
illustration in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#42263">Figure&nbsp;8-1</A>.=20
The shaded boxes indicate steps that physically retrieve data and the =
clear=20
boxes indicate steps that operate on data returned from the previous =
step.</P><A=20
name=3D42263></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
8-1 Execution=20
Plan with Two Full Table Scans</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D42267><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf023.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf023.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf023.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf023.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D42268></A>
<P class=3DBP>Step 3 of the output indicates that Oracle executes this =
statement=20
by performing a full table scan of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table despite =
the index=20
on the <CODE>deptno</CODE> column. This full table scan can be a =
time-consuming=20
operation. Oracle does not use the index, because the subquery that =
searches the=20
<CODE>emp</CODE> table does not have a <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause that =
makes the=20
index available.</P><A name=3D42273></A>
<P class=3DBP>However, this SQL statement selects the same rows by =
accessing the=20
index:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D42274></A>SELECT dname, deptno
<A name=3D42275></A>FROM dept
<A name=3D42276></A>WHERE NOT EXISTS
<A name=3D42277></A>    (SELECT deptno
<A name=3D42278></A>    FROM emp
<A name=3D42279></A>    WHERE dept.deptno =3D emp.deptno);
<A name=3D44261></A>
</PRE><A name=3D44264></A>
<P class=3DBP>The execution plan for the transformed statement might =
look like the=20
illustration in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#42280">Figure&nbsp;8-2</A>.=20
The shaded boxes indicate steps that physically retrieve data and the =
clear=20
boxes indicate steps that operate on data returned from the previous =
step.</P><A=20
name=3D42280></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
8-2 Execution=20
Plan with a Full Table Scan and an Index Scan</EM></FONT></H4><A =
name=3D42284><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf024.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf024.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf024.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf024.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D42285></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause of the subquery refers to =
the=20
<CODE>deptno</CODE> column of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table, so the index=20
<CODE>deptno_index</CODE> is used. The use of the index is reflected in =
step 3=20
of the execution plan. The index range scan of <CODE>deptno_index</CODE> =
takes=20
less time than the full scan of the <CODE>emp</CODE> table in the first=20
statement. Furthermore, the first query performs one full scan of the=20
<CODE>emp</CODE> table for every <CODE>deptno</CODE> in the =
<CODE>dept</CODE>=20
table. For these reasons, the second SQL statement is faster than the=20
first.</P><A name=3D42286></A>
<P class=3DBP>If you have statements in an application that use the=20
<CODE>NOT</CODE> <CODE>IN</CODE> operator, as the first query in this =
example=20
does, then consider rewriting them so that they use the <CODE>NOT</CODE> =

<CODE>EXISTS</CODE> operator. This allows such statements to use an =
index if one=20
exists.</P><A name=3D42291></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D42289></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D42290></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Alternative SQL syntax is effective only with the =
rule-based=20
      optimizer.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><!-- Start Footer --></DIV>
